    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d130">

                <pb facs="0293" n="197" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg197"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Day to Mr. Denby.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1898-06-06"><hi rendition="#italic">June 6,
                        1898</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1592.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch, No. 2916, of the 20th of April last, reporting that through an error
                    in a telegram to you from our consul-general at Shanghai you were led to demand
                        <pb facs="0294" n="198" xml:id="pg198"/> 500 taels instead of 5,000 taels
                    from the Chinese Government as indemnity for iujuries to Americans, resulting
                    from the riot at Chiang Pei. You add that you have informed the Yamên of the
                    mistake and have demanded the latter sum.</p>
                <p>Such mistakes suggest that, in telegraphing important sums, words and not figures
                    should be employed.</p>
                <p>Respectfully, yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William R. Day.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
